---
/**
 * Part D — prescription drug coverage.
 *
 * Its own page, cross-linked from both the Medigap page (where Part D is a
 * required companion) and the Advantage page (where it may already be built in).
 * The late-enrollment penalty is the reason this page exists, so it gets the
 * weight rather than a footnote.
 */
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { links, externalAttrs } from '../../data/links';

const title = 'Medicare Part D: Prescription Drug Coverage | Lisa’s Insurance Agency';
const description =
  'How Medicare Part D works, why the late-enrollment penalty is permanent, and how to match a drug plan to the medicines you actually take.';

const steps = [
  {
    title: 'Start With Your Medications',
    copy: 'Have your prescription bottles or medication list nearby to help find plan options that include the medications you take.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Pick Your Pharmacy',
    copy: 'Choose the pharmacy you prefer and see plans that include your pharmacy in their network.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Compare Your Plan Options',
    copy: 'Review available plans based on your medications, pharmacy, and coverage needs.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the late-enrollment penalty?',
    a: 'If you go without creditable prescription coverage for long enough after you’re first eligible for Part D, and later decide to enroll, an amount is added to your monthly premium. You keep paying it for as long as you have Part D. It grows with the length of the gap, so the longer the delay the larger it is.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What counts as creditable coverage?',
    a: 'Drug coverage that Medicare considers at least as good as a standard Part D plan, often coverage from an employer, a union, the VA, or TRICARE. If you have one of those, you can usually delay Part D with no penalty. Your plan is required to tell you each year whether it’s creditable; keep that notice.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I don’t take any medications. Do I still need it?',
    a: 'That’s the situation the penalty exists for. Signing up when you’re first eligible, even with nothing to fill, keeps the door open at no penalty later, and health changes without warning. It’s worth a conversation rather than a default assumption either way.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I have Part D with a Medicare Advantage plan?',
    a: 'Usually the Advantage plan already includes it, and if it does you generally can’t add a separate Part D plan. If your Advantage plan doesn’t include drug coverage, that’s a specific situation worth checking with me before you add anything.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I change plans if my prescriptions change?',
    a: 'Plans can change their drug lists each year, and so can your prescriptions, which is why it’s worth re-checking during the Annual Enrollment Period each autumn rather than letting a plan roll over unexamined. That review is part of what I do for clients, at no cost.',
  },
];
---

<Page
  title={title}
  description={description}
  rawTitle
  eyebrow="Medicare · Part D"
  heading="Prescription drug coverage."
  intro="Part D is how Medicare covers the medicines you pick up at a pharmacy. Understanding it now helps you plan for the future."
>
  <!-- ─── What Part D is ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section" id="what-it-is" aria-labelledby="what-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">What It Covers</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="what-heading">Prescription medications prescribed by your health care provider.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Original Medicare doesn’t cover most prescriptions you fill yourself.
          Part D provides drug coverage.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>What Is a Medicare Prescription Drug Plan?</h3>
        <p>
          A Medicare Prescription Drug Plan (Part D) helps pay for prescription
          medications and is offered by Medicare-approved private insurance
          companies. Prescription drug coverage can be purchased as a stand-alone
          plan to work with Original Medicare and a Medicare Supplement plan, or it
          can be included as part of a Medicare Advantage plan (Medicare Advantage
          Prescription Drug plan). Each plan has its own list of covered
          medications (formulary), costs, and pharmacy network.
        </p>

        <h3>Understanding Pharmacy Networks and Costs</h3>
        <p>
          Medicare prescription drug plans use a network of participating
          pharmacies. Many plans include both preferred pharmacies and standard
          network pharmacies. Using a preferred pharmacy may provide lower
          copayments, lower coinsurance, or other cost savings compared to a
          standard pharmacy. Your prescription costs can vary based on your
          medications, the plan’s drug tiers, and the pharmacy you choose, so it is
          important to review your plan’s pharmacy network and prescription
          coverage each year.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── The penalty ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section section--navy" id="penalty" aria-labelledby="penalty-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The penalty</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="penalty-heading">Understanding the Medicare Part D Late Enrollment Penalty</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          This is the single most expensive misunderstanding in Medicare, and it’s
          entirely avoidable.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          If you do not enroll in Medicare Part D when you are first eligible and go
          without creditable prescription drug coverage for 63 days or more, you may
          have to pay a late enrollment penalty if you enroll later. This penalty may
          be added to your monthly Part D premium and can continue as long as you
          have Medicare prescription drug coverage. Reviewing your options when you
          are first eligible can help you avoid potential additional costs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Already have drug coverage somewhere else?</h3>
        <p>
          Coverage through an employer, a union, the VA or TRICARE often counts as
          creditable, which means no penalty for delaying. Your plan has to tell you
          in writing each year whether it counts. That notice is worth keeping.
          Call me, and we’ll check what you’ve got.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="actions actions--stack">
        <a class="btn btn--accent" href={site.phoneHref}>Check my coverage · {site.phone}</a>
      </div>
      <p class="microline">Calling connects you with Lisa, a licensed insurance agent.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Enroll ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section" id="enroll" aria-labelledby="enroll-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Finding your plan</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="enroll-heading">Find a Plan That Fits Your Needs</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          3 Simple Steps to Compare Medicare Prescription Drug Plans
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps">
        {steps.map((step) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{step.title}</h3>
            <p>{step.copy}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <div class="medicare-cta">
        <p>
          Have your medications and pharmacy information ready? Compare plans online
          or contact me for help reviewing your options to enroll by phone. The plan
          premium and benefits are the same either way.
        </p>

        <div class="actions actions--stack">
          <a class="btn btn--accent" href={links.sunfirePartD} {...externalAttrs}>
            Simply Enroll
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact">Questions? Contact Lisa</a>
        </div>
        <p class="microline">Calling connects you with Lisa, a licensed insurance agent.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── FAQ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section section--navy" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="faq-heading">Questions about Part D.</h2>
      </div>

      <Faq items={faqs} schema />

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top: clamp(40px, 5vw, 64px)">
        <h3>How Will Prescription Coverage Work With My Medicare Plan?</h3>
        <p>
          Whether you have Original Medicare with a Medicare Supplement, are
          considering Medicare Advantage, or are exploring your options, it’s
          important to understand how prescription drug coverage fits into your
          plan.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Have questions or need assistance?"
    copy="I’m easy to reach and ready to help you review your Medicare options."
  />
</Page>
